A||N||8||truxpin||87 GT -- front or rear wheel drive?? need to tow||||5||
Z||000000||truxpin||09-24-2001||09:18 PM||||OK, I guess this is an easy question for those that actually own a fiero! But , this week-end I'm going 650 miles to pick up my first one. I was wanting to drive it home but have to make the trip alone. Was thinking about getting one of those U-Haul front wheel dollies to haul it with, but I don't know if they are FWD or RWD??? <BR>Does anyone know if you can get one on a full size U-Haul car hauler?  Did'nt know if they sit to low and would scrape the hauler or not.<BR>Any help would be appreciated, Thanks a bunch<P>------------------<BR>Hopefully I can put a pic of my '87 GT in here soon!!<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/royalstartourdeluxe/webbike.JPG"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Master_Sushi||09-24-2001||09:30 PM||||All fieros are mid engine and rear drive. The way all cars should be, well rear drive at least.||||reg||12
Z||000002||Rodrv6||09-24-2001||09:32 PM||||It's rear wheel drive.  The Fiero should fit on a regular car trailer unless the previous owner has lowered the car.  Take it slow and load it carefully and it should be no problem.<BR>Oh, and welcome to the Forum!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting, 88 GT, not nearly as many miles :)||||reg||1
Z||000003||gt boy||09-24-2001||09:37 PM||||truxpin..i am glad you asked the question about putting the car on a u-haul(the full size one not the dolley).  I just picked up two cars with the full size trailer and had two problems, my one car had the coolant pipe gouged and leaked all the coolant out and the other car i picked up had the tranny lines ripped off.  now this could have been due to loose lines or something, but it was a pain in the @$$ just the same.<P>BTW the cars were not lowered.<P>i highly suggest the 2 wheel dolley if you can get one...and tow by the back wheels.<BR><P>------------------<BR>"it's too bad he had to run out of talent half way through the corner..."||||reg||1
Z||000004||jelly2m8||09-24-2001||09:40 PM||||If the car is a automatic, tow it backwards with the drive wheels on the car dolly. If it is a standard transaxale, you can tow it either way.<P> The best method is with a full deck trailer like you mentioned.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||09-25-2001||04:51 AM||||I towed my second Fiero ('85 GT/auto) about 3 1/2 hours on a dolly.  Towed it backwards with the car in park.  No problems at all.  There are others (007Doug, Bob Stieger?, Ed Parks) who tow Fieros on a semi regular basis and use dollies all the time.  Save the money and the hassles and just get a dolly.  Remember to tow an auto backwards and a stick either way (make sure it's in neutral if you're towing it forwards).  Be sure to make sure the trunks (both) and sunroof are latched well and make sure that you secure (lock) the steering wheel (don't count on the steering column lock to hold for 650 miles--wrap the seatbelt around the wheel [with it all the way down if a tilt column], then latch the belt).  Wheels straight.  You'll be fine.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Wolfhound||09-25-2001||08:25 AM||||You can tow a standard car either way but it will tow better backward. It,s better with engine weight forward.<BR>Coy||||reg||1
Z||000007||pontiacs4me||09-26-2001||03:06 AM||||I dont care which way you tow it, just make sure you get it back to wichita safe.  We need some more nice fieros around here, most of them are either in junkyards or need to be.||||reg||1
Z||000008||StuGood||09-26-2001||12:53 PM||||Heeyyyy - don't make fun of my cars, pontiacs4me  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  Just because my place looks like a junkyard  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">        <P>Yep - stock Fieros are all RWD.  FWIW, I hauled a Fiero in backwards using a spare seat belt, about a mile.  That was kind of scary.  I am about to acquire another one, from about 20 miles out, and several people told me they had good luck with one of those dollys.<P>650 miles? Wow! Be careful, and good luck with it. I agree - we can always use another Fiero to admire here in Wichita  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||14
